# nr-office-formula-lib-financial

## Registration (lead-owned functions/mod.rs)

Add `mod financial;` after the existing library module declarations.
Append `financial::FUNCTIONS,` to TABLES, preserving other tasks' entries.
The table contains all 33 Financial-category exported paths discovered from the
installed @formulajs/formulajs 4.6.1 namespace. Its actual exports end at XNPV;
YIELD*, ACCRINTM and the other absent spreadsheet names mentioned by the plan are
not exports or stubs in this installed version and are not fabricated here.

No new crate or Cargo edge. Existing coerce, mixdog-js V8 math and radix helpers
are reused. Calendar prerequisites, resolved from the production registry:
YEARFRAC, DATEDIF, DAYS360, DAYS (owned by nr-office-formula-lib-text-date).
Register those before financial_calendar_results_equal_original_bits. The pure
financial test needs no datetime table. Neither test silently skips a missing
function; together they cover every normal recorded output.

Remove the ignore attribute from financial_exports_are_registered_in_the_production_library
when the production table is wired. twin_live_financial stays opt-in because it
runs Node and the bounded nontermination diagnostic.

## Source and output coverage

Implementation: mixdog-xlsx-formula/src/functions/financial.rs
Original: node_modules/@formulajs/formulajs/lib/esm/index.mjs, 11473-13050,
plus validDate/ensureDate/lastCoupDateBeforeSettlement immediately before it.
Recorder enumerates Financial JSDoc function identities against the actual export
namespace and traverses nested aliases, rejecting any unhandled export.

Recorded 3,299 returning cases, all 33 exports, original Node 22.20.0.
Each case preserves float bits (NaN/sign/payload included), Error identity label,
throw message, undefined/null/arrays/dates and time zone. No tolerance or NaN
normalization. Numeric/date inputs are encoded before calling the mutation-prone
reference. Dates include UTC, America/New_York and Asia/Seoul. Coverage includes
missing arguments, truthy vs undefined defaults, bad types, multiple errors,
raw/string CUM periods, nonfinite values where the original terminates,
DB's toFixed(3), scientific-notation parseInt conversions, all day-count bases,
COUPDAYS overflowed month ends, IRR/RATE/XIRR guesses and cashflow shapes.

Node command (executed, reference read-only, outputs only owned fixtures):
node benchmarks/rust-core/recorders/record-office-financial.mjs --tree C:/Project/mixdog --write
Without --write the recorder emits JSON and does not change fixtures.
Index office-financial/MANIFEST.json in the root fixture manifest. It carries the
source SHA-256, exact export roster, case count and golden byte/hash metadata.

Strict tests:
- every_original_financial_export_has_one_body
- financial_noncalendar_results_equal_original_bits
- financial_calendar_results_equal_original_bits
- financial_exports_are_registered_in_the_production_library (enable after wiring)
- twin_live_financial (opt-in Node rerecord/compare)

No Rust compilation/tests, applications, dependency downloads or commits were run.
Rustfmt parsed/formatted the source only. Exact numerical replay is NOT declared
verified; root must compile, run both replay partitions and clippy offline.

## Iteration behavior and required process containment

IRR preserves its 10-decimal SameValueZero NPV cache, manual power accumulation,
1000 Newton iterations, original bracket expansion and 1000 bisection iterations.
RATE preserves 100 iterations and its two small-rate derivative branches.
XIRR has NO iteration count in the installed source; its AND-based epsilon/value
termination is preserved. CUM/DB/DDB preserve their original period loops.

An actual original IRR(cashflows, +Infinity) call did not terminate within the
recorder's 1-second VM limit. The source's bisection lower bound stays Infinity
when subtracting 0.1. The recorder verifies this as a separate nontermination
item, not a financial output/error and not an unbounded test case. Raw +Infinity
CUM end periods are likewise outside the bounded output corpus because the
source's period loop cannot end. No new production limit/refusal was invented.

Containment found in this base (read directly):
- mixdog-tools/src/orch/office.rs:192 sends office.run via ExtPool; cancellation
  drops its call stream.
- mixdog-exthost/src/stream.rs:57 Drop reports the abandoned call to the pool.
- mixdog-exthost/src/pool.rs:54 CANCEL_GRACE is 5 seconds; on_dropped sends CANCEL
  while retaining the busy slot; CancelGrace at ~408 calls stop(io), which at
  ~632 calls io.kill.kill(), ending the owned worker process/job/group.
- Current original src/ext-host/methods.mjs officeRun invokes executeOfficeTool;
  portable/xlsx-recalculate.mjs synchronously evaluates formula cells in that
  worker process, so even an event-loop-blocking formula is externally killable.
- Native mixdog-exthost-worker/src/registry.rs::register_blocking runs synchronous
  work on a worker-process thread. run.rs CANCEL cancels the link token but does
  not synthesize RESULT while its handler is still running. Thus an uncooperative
  formula leaves the pool slot Busy and the same grace can kill the child.

In this base, no production Rust caller of mixdog_xlsx_formula was found outside
the crate/tests, and office.run is NOT yet in native_methods (only document/image
methods). Therefore native financial-loop containment is not yet exercised.
Mandatory integration: native Office recalculation must execute inside that
owned ext-worker process, with the blocking registry path and pool cancellation
retained. Never call these synchronous potentially nonterminating formulas on a
daemon task/thread (including daemon spawn_blocking), which cannot be killed by
this pool boundary. A cooperative cancellation API may be added separately if
approved; it is not a replacement for the owned process boundary.
Before enabling native office.run, exercise cancellation of the actual IRR
Infinity case in an isolated child and verify its PID is gone after grace; do
not run it directly in a unit-test/daemon process. Existing pool regression
`a_parser_that_ignores_the_cancel_is_ended_after_the_grace` covers the mechanism
but is not evidence that the future native Office handler is wired through it.

## Incoming shared UTF-16 Value integration

This base has Value::Text(String), not the additive Value::Utf16 contract.
Do not change shared Value/coerce concurrently. On integration, update:
- financial.rs::primitive: Array/Error default string primitives must use
  V::from_utf16(coerce::to_js_utf16(value)), not lossy to_js_string.
- financial.rs::less/less_equal: compare is_text()/text_units() lexicographically
  when both operands are text; raw CUM period comparisons preserve JS string order.
- financial.rs::ensure_date: explicit V::Utf16 text arm must follow Date(string),
  not fall through to numeric milliseconds. Use the shared raw-text date parsing
  contract; retain non-well-formed inputs rather than silently sanitizing them.
- tests/twin_financial.rs::encode is an exhaustive Value match and must gain a
  Utf16/raw-code-unit encoding arm when that variant lands. Decoder should use
  Value::from_utf16 for future raw-unit fixture input. Existing recorded financial
  outputs are numeric/error; no surrogate mismatch has been waived.
